<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Czechoslovak New Wave</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1960s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Czechoslovak New Wave was a movement of young film-makers in the 1960s, among them Miloš Forman, Věra Chytilová and Jiří Menzel. Using irony, improvisation and experiment, their films questioned the official culture of communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. The Shop on Main Street and Closely Watched Trains won Academy Awards, and the movement ended with the crushing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name covers the Czechoslovak film-makers who began making films in the 1960s, a movement sometimes called the Czechoslovak film miracle. Besides Miloš Forman, Věra Chytilová and Jiří Menzel, the directors usually counted in it include Jan Švankmajer, Ivan Passer, Pavel Juráček, Jan Němec, Jaromil Jireš, Evald Schorm, Hynek Bočan, Juraj Herz, Juraj Jakubisko, Štefan Uher and František Vláčil. Many came from FAMU, the film school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, and worked at the state-run Barrandov Studios on the city's outskirts. Most of their films were in Czech, but Herz, Jakubisko, Uher and Dušan Hanák were Slovak members of the movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The films took up subjects that earlier film-makers in communist countries had rarely shown without objections from the censors: the misguided youth of Forman's Black Peter (1963) and Loves of a Blonde (1965), or the surreal whirl of Chytilová's Daisies (1966) and Jireš's Valerie and Her Week of Wonders (1970). Against the socialist realist films of the 1950s they set a dark and absurd humour.</p><p>The movement differed from the French New Wave in favouring stronger narratives. Because its directors worked within a nationalised film industry, they had greater access to studios and state money, and they made more adaptations of literature, among them Jireš's film of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-kundera">Milan Kundera</a>'s novel The Joke. Other works of the period include Vláčil's Marketa Lazarová (1967), Němec's A Report on the Party and the Guests (1966) and Herz's The Cremator (1969).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>At the Fourth Congress of the Czechoslovak Writers Union in 1967, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-kundera">Milan Kundera</a> described this cinema as an important part of the history of Czechoslovak literature. Menzel's Closely Watched Trains won the Academy Award for Best Foreign Language Film in 1968. Forman's The Firemen's Ball (1967) has remained a cult film for more than four decades after its release.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Czechoslovak New Wave” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cultural life was rich: Karel Čapek, Leoš Janáček, Jaroslav Hašek, the <b>Czechoslovak New Wave</b> in film and later Milan Kundera and Václav Havel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
